 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "An AOSD NGS cohort (24 patients) in which one identifiable patient carries a TNFAIP3 VUS: Table 2 lists \"Patient 4 | TNFAIP3 (c.1939A>C)\" with individual sex, features and treatment response; she has no HA20 diagnosis (diagnosis is AOSD).",
 "method_note": "Read the whole paper manually; a short Python script only assembled the JSON. Only Patient 4 (Table 2) is in scope; the other 23 cohort members (incl. Patients 1-3 and 5 with NOD2, TNFRSF1A and SCN9A VUS) get no rows. Table 2 has patients as rows; spans quote the row from the label through the cell. The protein description T647P comes from Table 3, column 'Present study (2024)', cell 'TNFAIP3: T647P'; it is attributed to Patient 4 because the text states only one patient carried a TNFAIP3 variant ('in another patient in TNFAIP3 (c.1939A>C)'). It is printed without a 'p.' prefix and recorded as printed. Table 2 gives 'Female, 60 years' without saying which age this is (diagnosis, onset or time of report), so all age fields are left 'not reported'. Patient 4's feature list in Table 2 says 'Fever' without stating it was recurrent, so the checklist item 'recurrent fever' is left 'not reported' (the Fever phenotypic_features row is kept). Elevated acute-phase reactants and hyperferritinemia are inferred from the explicit whole-cohort statement 'all patients had elevated acute-phase reactant levels and hyperferritinemia'. Counts without names (e.g. ANA positive n = 2, mucocutaneous ulcers 6/24) were not used for anyone. The Discussion also places this patient in AOSD 'cluster 2 or the “uncomplicated” group' and says she 'did not show atypical symptoms'; not recorded as features. Inconsistencies noted: the Discussion writes 'the patient with the TNFAIP variant' (gene symbol missing the 3) for the same patient; Methods say diagnosis was by Yamaguchi and/or Fautrel criteria, while Results say all 24 'fulfilled the Yamaguchi criteria'. No variant type, zygosity, inheritance, parental testing or functional data are given for the TNFAIP3 variant.",
